.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-setup 1  "tapectl-setup " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-setup \- Set up tapectl for one drive on your own account, step by step
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl setup\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-staging\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-generation\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-operator\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-escrow\-public\-key\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-location\fR] [\fB\-\-collection\-root\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-collection\fR] [\fB\-\-tenant\fR] [\fB\-\-backup\-dir\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-timers\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Set up tapectl for one drive on your own account, step by step
.PP
For one person with one tape drive on their own Linux account (a host with a service user is set up by scripts/first\-run.sh instead). Each step says what it is about to do, then what it did: the home (~/.tapectl unless \-\-home names another); a staging directory; the drive, found under /dev/tape/by\-id/ with its generation read from its model (the tape is never opened); the operator\*(Aqs keys and a new escrow identity, whose secret is printed once on stderr, as `init` prints it (under \-\-yes too), to copy onto paper, and stored nowhere; two locations; a first collection, its units registered on yes; weekly catalog\-backup and audit timers as systemd user units (files only); `config check`. Then what needs root, as one block for you to run (setup never runs sudo), and the commands for the first two copies.
.PP
A step already done is skipped, so a run that stopped is finished by running setup again, and a run on a finished home changes nothing. Each question has its flag; \-\-yes takes every default and refuses a question that has none, naming its flag; \-\-dry\-run says what each step would do and changes nothing. Without a terminal, setup needs \-\-yes or \-\-dry\-run.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-staging\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Directory for staged slices: ideally a big disk that is not the source

Created when it does not exist (setup asks first). Default: the staging directory config.toml already names, `<home>/staging` in a new home.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
The tape drive\*(Aqs node, preferably its /dev/tape/by\-id/…\-nst link

Default: the one drive under /dev/tape/by\-id/; with several, setup asks which. A /dev/nstN is written as the by\-id link that names it, when one does. Only the drive\*(Aqs identity is read (from the kernel\*(Aqs records); the tape is never opened.
.TP
\fB\-\-generation\fR \fI<GEN>\fR
The generation the drive writes, e.g. LTO\-6 (default: its model\*(Aqs)

As for `drive add`: needed when the drive is not attached now or its model is not one tapectl knows.
.TP
\fB\-\-operator\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
The operator\*(Aqs name (default: this account\*(Aqs login name)
.TP
\fB\-\-escrow\-public\-key\fR \fI<KEY_OR_FILE>\fR
Adopt this escrow public key instead of minting a new escrow identity

An age1… literal, or a path to a .pub file, as for `init \-\-escrow\-public\-key`. Left out, setup mints one as `init` does: its secret is printed once on stderr (under \-\-yes too, terminal or not), to copy onto paper, and stored nowhere (ADR\-0005).
.TP
\fB\-\-location\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
A place cartridges are kept; give it twice (default: home, offsite)
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\-root\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
The folder whose subfolders are the units, e.g. /media/photos

The first collection\*(Aqs root. It has no default: under \-\-yes, setup refuses without it.
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
The first collection\*(Aqs name (default: its folder\*(Aqs name)
.TP
\fB\-\-tenant\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
The tenant the collection\*(Aqs units belong to (default: the operator)

Added when it does not exist.
.TP
\fB\-\-backup\-dir\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Directory for the weekly catalog backup, best on another disk

Default: ~/tapectl\-backups (setup warns when it is on the home\*(Aqs disk).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-timers\fR
Write no systemd user timers for the backup and the audit
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
.SH EXTRA
EXAMPLES:
  Answer the questions one at a time (Enter takes the default in brackets):
    tapectl setup

  Take every default, naming what has none:
    tapectl setup \-\-yes \-\-staging /srv/staging \-\-device /dev/tape/by\-id/<DRIVE>\-nst \\
        \-\-collection\-root /media/photos

  See what each step would do, and change nothing:
    tapectl setup \-\-dry\-run

Exit status: 0 = every step is done (a second run on a finished home says so and
changes nothing); 2 = a step was refused or failed: what was done before it stays
done, and running setup again finishes the rest.
